; 39-chain.kurt

; `chain` lets mixed (in)equalities be written the way mathematicians write
; them, e.g. `x = y < z` instead of `x = y and y < z`.  `chain OP1 OP2 ...`
; declares which infix operators may be strung together this way, and in
; which order; the loaded theories already declare the usual ones (`=`,
; `<=`, `<`, and `iff`/`implies` for one-directional reasoning).

load equality
infix < 20 20
bool  < 0
chain = <              ; `=` may be followed by `<` in a chain

var x, y, z
use x = y
use y < z

; an indented line starting with a chainable operator continues from the
; previous line's right-hand side -- so this desugars to `x < z`:
x = y                   ; the first step, as always
  < z                   ; continuing the chain

; On a single line, chained relations mean a conjunction: `x = y < z` is
; `x = y and y < z` (parentheses, `(x = y) < z`, switch that off):
x = y < z

; A chain must be indented to continue (see `doc/dev-notes.md`, "chains");
; writing the continuation at the same indentation as the first line is a
; `ParseError`, not a chain.
